# Question: what availability does a service get from the pieces it runs on,
# some in series and some in parallel, and how likely is it to meet a 99.9%
# target?
#
# Run it (it uses the shared formulas):
#   ubc --scenario reliability/availability
# Change an assumption, for example a managed database that is down much less:
#   ubc --set 'database_down = 0.01% to 0.05%' --scenario reliability/availability
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens availability --scenario reliability/availability
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Each piece is described by
# the share of the time it is down, because a range of availabilities such
# as 99.9% to 99.99% would put some samples above 100%. A request needs the
# load balancer, one of two application instances and the database: the
# pieces in series multiply, the two instances are in parallel. It assumes
# the pieces fail independently; a shared cause (a bad deploy, a zone
# outage) takes both instances down at once and makes it worse.
#
# Sources: Betsy Beyer et al., Site Reliability Engineering (Google, 2016,
# sre.google/books), for availability "nines"; the downtime ranges are typical
# experience.

# Assumptions
balancer_down = 0.01% to 0.05%    # share of the time the load balancer is down; see its provider's SLA
instance_down = 0.2% to 2%        # time one instance is down, deploys included; typical experience, see your monitoring
database_down = 0.05% to 0.3%     # time the single database is down; typical experience, see your monitoring
minutes_per_month = days_per_month * hours_per_calendar_day * minutes_per_hour   # a 30-day month

# Calculation
balancer = 1 - balancer_down                                                # availability of each piece
instance = 1 - instance_down
database = 1 - database_down
application = availability_parallel(instance, instance)                     # either instance is enough
availability = availability_series(balancer, availability_series(application, database))   # all three steps are needed
downtime_minutes = (1 - availability) * minutes_per_month                   # minutes down a month
nines = -log10(1 - availability)                                            # 3 nines is 99.9%

# Result
nines
downtime_minutes
P(availability > 99.9%)           # how likely is the service to meet a 99.9% availability target?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   nines = ≈ 2.8   90% [2.46 … 3.11]   98% [2.29 … 3.23]   ▁▂▂▃▄▅▇████▆▅▃▃▂
#   downtime_minutes = ≈ 68.6   90% [33.5 … 151]   98% [25 … 220]   ▄▇█▇▆▅▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁
#   P(availability > 99.9%) = 0.14
